  "response": "## The finding\n\n`crime_00017` — **Dev-Server URL** (`hardcoded_localhost`), `src/api/files.ts:8`, severity medium, confidence 0.90, agent_risk 0.30, effort \"quick\".\n\nThe literal quoted in the evidence is:\n\n> ``` `localhost:3000` @L8 ```\n\nIn the source that's the full constant the PR adds:\n\n```ts\nconst ADMIN_DASHBOARD = \"http://localhost:3000/admin\";\nexport function adminUrl(): string { return ADMIN_DASHBOARD; }\n```\n\n## Verdict: request changes, don't block\n\nBlocking is over-escalation. `blast_radius` is 0 — `adminUrl()` has no importers yet, so nothing in the repo currently renders this link, and the fix is a `quick` one-liner plus a config file. There's no security exposure in the literal itself (no credentials, no internal hostname worth leaking).\n\nBut it's also not a nit you wave through, for one reason: this constant fails *silently*. There's no error when `http://localhost:3000/admin` ships to staging or prod — an admin clicks the link and either gets a dead tab or, worse, lands on whatever service happens to be bound to port 3000 on their own machine while authenticated in an admin context. A URL that's wrong everywhere except one laptop should not enter the default branch, and it's cheaper to fix now than after three call sites copy it.\n\nTwo things push this from \"comment\" to \"changes requested\":\n\n1. **The same PR block also hardcodes `/Users/andrew/dev/crimes/schema.json` at line 9** (`crime_00018`, same file, same class). That path exists on exactly one machine — every other dev and every CI runner gets `loadPlanSchema()` returning `null` from the `existsSync` guard, quietly. Fix both in one pass.\n2. Unused exported constants are the seed stock for duplication. This repo already has `magic_domain_literal_scatter` on `\"enterprise\"` across four files — same failure mode, one generation later.\n\n## Where the URL should go\n\nThe generic advice (\"`process.env.API_URL`, a settings module, or the framework's runtime config\") needs narrowing, because two of those three don't apply here. `package.json` declares zero dependencies, `\"type\": \"module\"`, and a `bin` entry — **there is no framework, so there is no runtime-config slot to use.** No Next.js `publicRuntimeConfig`, no Vite `import.meta.env`. Ruling that out is part of the recommendation.\n\nSo: **env var read once through a new settings module.**\n\n**Env var name:** `ADMIN_DASHBOARD_URL`. Not `API_URL` — this isn't the API base, it's a specific dashboard origin, and a generic name invites the next person to overload it.\n\n**Settings module location:** `src/config/env.ts`. The repo has `src/api/`, `src/jobs/`, `src/nav/`, `src/routes/`, `src/ui/` and no config directory — this creates the missing one at the layer everything else can import downward from.\n\n```ts\n// src/config/env.ts — the only place process.env is read.\nfunction required(name: string, devDefault?: string): string {\n  const value = process.env[name] ?? devDefault;\n  if (!value) throw new Error(`Missing required environment variable: ${name}`);\n  return value;\n}\n\nexport const config = Object.freeze({\n  adminDashboardUrl: required(\n    \"ADMIN_DASHBOARD_URL\",\n    process.env.NODE_ENV === \"production\" ? undefined : \"http://localhost:3000/admin\",\n  ),\n  schemaPath: required(\"PLAN_SCHEMA_PATH\"),\n});\n```\n\n```ts\n// src/api/files.ts\nimport { config } from \"../config/env.js\";\nexport function adminUrl(): string {\n  return config.adminDashboardUrl;\n}\n```\n\nThe dev default is deliberately conditional: localhost stays convenient locally, and production **throws at startup** instead of shipping a link to a dev box. Fail loud beats fail silent — that's the whole defect being fixed, so don't reintroduce it as an unconditional fallback.\n\n**Supporting changes:**\n- Commit `.env.example` with `ADMIN_DASHBOARD_URL=http://localhost:3000/admin` and `PLAN_SCHEMA_PATH=./schema.json`; gitignore `.env.local`.\n- For line 9, prefer resolving from the package root or an explicit `PLAN_SCHEMA_PATH` over `os.homedir()` — the schema is repo data, not user data, so a home-relative path is the wrong shape even when it's portable.\n- Keep `process.env` reads confined to `src/config/env.ts`. Scattered reads are the thing that makes this class of bug recur, and centralizing gives tests one seam to override.\n\n**One caveat to check before merging:** if `adminUrl()` will ever be called from the `.tsx` files under `src/routes/`, the value crosses into a browser bundle and a plain server-side env var won't reach it — it would need the bundler's public prefix (`NEXT_PUBLIC_ADMIN_DASHBOARD_URL` / `VITE_ADMIN_DASHBOARD_URL`) and must stay free of anything secret. As written the helper lives in `src/api/` and is server-side, so the plain variable above is correct today.",
